```

The buffer is allocated with size `nlen.uns + 1`. The expansion writes at most `nlen.uns` characters (plus the null terminator at the end). But wait, where is the null terminator written?

Looking at the code:
```c
/* Nuke the trailing period if we wrote one. */
if (q > *s)
    *(q - 1) = 0;
else
    *q = 0; /* zero terminate; LCOV_EXCL_LINE: empty names exit above */
```

The null terminator is written at `q - 1` (overwriting the trailing dot) or at `q` (if no labels were written). But `q` has been incremented for each character written. So the null terminator is written within the allocated buffer.

Wait, but what if `q` has been incremented past the end of the buffer? This would happen if `name_length` undercounts the expansion length.

Let me think about when `name_length` could undercount...

In `name_length`:
```c
n += (*encoded == '.' || *encoded == '\\') ? 2 : 1;
```

This counts 2 for '.' and '\\'. In `ares_expand_name`:
```c
if (*p == '.' || *p == '\\')
    *q++ = '\\';
*q++ = *p;
```

This writes 2 bytes for '.' and '\\'. So the counting matches.

